<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter5_constructors">
  <title>Constructors</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In the previous section, a <c>Rectangle</c> object first received
      default values and then used <c>set_dimensions</c> to establish useful
      dimensions.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room;
room.set_dimensions(12.0, 8.0);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      It would be better if an object could receive its intended starting
      state at the moment it is created.
    </p>

    <p>
      C++ provides a special member function for this purpose: a
      <term>constructor</term>.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>What Is a Constructor?</title>

    <p>
      A constructor is a special member function that is called automatically
      when an object is created.
    </p>

    <p>
      A constructor has the same name as its class and has no return type.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
public:
    Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width);

private:
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The declaration
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      declares a constructor for <c>Rectangle</c>. Notice that there is no
      return type, not even <c>void</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      When an object is created with matching arguments, C++ calls the
      constructor automatically:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Here, <c>12.0</c> and <c>8.0</c> are passed to the constructor as
      <c>initial_length</c> and <c>initial_width</c>.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Defining a Constructor</title>

    <p>
      Like other member functions, a constructor can be declared inside the
      class and defined later.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
public:
    Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width);

private:
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The definition uses the scope resolution operator:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle::Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width) {
    length = initial_length;
    width = initial_width;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The name appears twice:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle::Rectangle
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The first <c>Rectangle</c> identifies the class whose member is being
      defined. The second <c>Rectangle</c> is the name of the constructor.
    </p>

    <p>
      A constructor does not return an object with a <c>return</c> statement.
      C++ creates the object and then runs the constructor to initialize it.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Member Initializer Lists</title>

    <p>
      C++ provides a preferred syntax for initializing data members in a
      constructor: a <term>member initializer list</term>.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle::Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width)
    : length(initial_length), width(initial_width) {
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The initializer list appears after the constructor's parameter list and
      before the constructor body.
    </p>

    <p>
      In this example:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
length(initial_length)
width(initial_width)
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      initialize the data members <c>length</c> and <c>width</c> from the
      constructor parameters.
    </p>

    <p>
      For straightforward member initialization, prefer an initializer list
      rather than first creating the members and then assigning values inside
      the constructor body.
    </p>

    <p>
      The parameter names <c>initial_length</c> and <c>initial_width</c> also
      make it clear which names are parameters and which names are data
      members.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Calling a Constructor</title>

    <p>
      We can create a <c>Rectangle</c> and pass constructor arguments using
      braces:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};
Rectangle table{6.0, 3.0};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Each statement creates a separate object and invokes the constructor for
      that object.
    </p>

    <p>
      Parentheses can also be used for this kind of construction:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room(12.0, 8.0);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      In this book, we will generally prefer brace initialization:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Earlier, braces were used to initialize simple structures directly.
      Once a class provides constructors, braces can also be used to select
      and call a matching constructor.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>A Default Constructor</title>

    <p>
      A constructor that can be called with no arguments is called a
      <term>default constructor</term>.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
public:
    Rectangle();
    Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width);

private:
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      We can define the default constructor so that a newly created rectangle
      begins as a 1-by-1 rectangle:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle::Rectangle()
    : length(1.0), width(1.0) {
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Now this declaration calls the default constructor:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle unit_square;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Braces may also be written explicitly:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle unit_square{};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Both forms create a <c>Rectangle</c> using the no-argument constructor.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Constructor Overloading</title>

    <p>
      A class can have more than one constructor as long as the parameter
      lists allow C++ to determine which constructor to call.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
public:
    Rectangle();
    Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width);

private:
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      These two constructors have the same name but different parameter lists.
      This is called <term>constructor overloading</term>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The arguments used when an object is created determine which constructor
      is selected:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle unit_square;        // calls Rectangle()
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};    // calls Rectangle(double, double)
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Constructor overloading is one example of the more general idea of
      <term>function overloading</term>. We will study function overloading in
      more detail later in the book. For now, the important idea is that a
      class may provide more than one sensible way to create an object.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Constructors and Object State</title>

    <p>
      A constructor gives a class control over the state an object has when it
      first comes into existence.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, the default constructor below creates a sensible 1-by-1
      rectangle:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle::Rectangle()
    : length(1.0), width(1.0) {
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      For the constructor that receives dimensions, we can also decide what
      should happen if the requested values are not valid for our model.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle::Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width)
    : length(1.0), width(1.0) {
    if (initial_length > 0.0 && initial_width > 0.0) {
        length = initial_length;
        width = initial_width;
    }
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      This version begins with a valid 1-by-1 rectangle. If both requested
      dimensions are positive, the constructor replaces those defaults with
      the requested values. Otherwise, the object remains 1 by 1.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is one possible design policy. Different classes may choose
      different policies for invalid construction requests. We will return to
      those design decisions when we discuss an object's interface and
      behavior.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>A Complete Example</title>

    <p>
      The following program shows constructor declarations inside the class,
      object creation in <c>main</c>, and constructor definitions after
      <c>main</c>.
    </p>

    <listing label="chapter5_constructors_AC_1">
      <title>
        Two overloaded constructors provide two ways to create a
        <c>Rectangle</c>.
      </title>

      <program interactive="activecode"
               line-numbers="yes"
               label="chapter5_constructors_AC_1_editor">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Rectangle {
public:
    Rectangle();
    Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width);

    double calculate_area() const;

private:
    double length;
    double width;
};

int main() {
    Rectangle unit_square;
    Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

    std::cout << unit_square.calculate_area() << '\n';
    std::cout << room.calculate_area() << '\n';

    return 0;
}

Rectangle::Rectangle()
    : length(1.0), width(1.0) {
}

Rectangle::Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width)
    : length(1.0), width(1.0) {
    if (initial_length > 0.0 && initial_width > 0.0) {
        length = initial_length;
        width = initial_width;
    }
}

double Rectangle::calculate_area() const {
    return length * width;
}
        ]]></code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      The first object uses the default constructor, so its area is
      <c>1.0</c>. The second object uses the two-parameter constructor, so its
      area is <c>96.0</c>.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise label="chapter5_constructors_1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which declaration correctly declares a constructor for a class named
          <c>Book</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>void Book();</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A constructor has no return type, not even <c>void</c>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>Book();</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A constructor has the same name as the class and no return type.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>book();</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Constructor names must match the class name exactly.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>Book Book();</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A constructor does not specify a return type.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_constructors_2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Given these constructor declarations, which constructor is called by
          <c>Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};</c>?
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle();
Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width);
        ]]></program>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>Rectangle()</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The no-argument constructor does not match the two arguments
              supplied when <c>room</c> is created.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><c>Rectangle(double initial_length, double initial_width)</c></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The two supplied <c>double</c> values match the two-parameter
              constructor.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p>Both constructors</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              One matching constructor is selected for the construction.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_constructors_3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the purpose of the initializer list in this constructor?
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
Book::Book(const std::string& initial_title, int initial_year)
    : title(initial_title), year(initial_year) {
}
        ]]></program>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              It initializes the data members from the constructor arguments.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The initializer list initializes <c>title</c> and <c>year</c>
              as the object is constructed.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              It declares two new local variables named <c>title</c> and
              <c>year</c>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>title</c> and <c>year</c> are data members of the object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              It returns the completed <c>Book</c> object.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Constructors do not return objects with a return statement.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_constructors_4"
              number="yes"
              adaptive="yes"
              indentation="hide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Arrange the blocks to define the two-parameter constructor for
          <c>Book</c> outside the class using a member initializer list.
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
class Book {
public:
    Book(const std::string& initial_title, int initial_year);

private:
    std::string title;
    int year;
};
        ]]></program>
      </statement>

      <blocks>
        <block order="1">
          <cline>Book::Book(const std::string&amp; initial_title, int initial_year)</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="2">
          <cline>    : title(initial_title), year(initial_year) {</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="3">
          <cline>}</cline>
        </block>

        <block correct="no">
          <cline>void Book::Book(const std::string&amp; initial_title, int initial_year)</cline>
        </block>

        <block correct="no">
          <cline>    return Book;</cline>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_constructors_5">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Complete the two constructor definitions for <c>Book</c>. The
          default constructor should create an untitled book from year 0. The
          second constructor should initialize the object from its arguments.
        </p>

        <program interactive="activecode"
                 line-numbers="yes"
                 label="chapter5_constructors_5_editor">
          <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Book {
public:
    Book();
    Book(const std::string& initial_title, int initial_year);

    void report() const;

private:
    std::string title;
    int year;
};

int main() {
    Book unknown;
    Book novel{"The Left Hand of Darkness", 1969};

    unknown.report();
    novel.report();

    return 0;
}

Book::Book()
    // Complete the initializer list.
{
}

Book::Book(const std::string& initial_title, int initial_year)
    // Complete the initializer list.
{
}

void Book::report() const {
    std::cout << title << " (" << year << ")\n";
}
          ]]></code>
        </program>
      </statement>

      <hint>
        <p>
          Initialize <c>title</c> and <c>year</c> after a colon and before the
          constructor body.
        </p>
      </hint>

      <solution>
        <program><![CDATA[
Book::Book()
    : title("Untitled"), year(0) {
}

Book::Book(const std::string& initial_title, int initial_year)
    : title(initial_title), year(initial_year) {
}
        ]]></program>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

</section>
